{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9653dffb9cb774dd", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# LibriTTS: A Corpus Derived from LibriSpeech for Text-to-Speech\narXiv: 1904.02882\nAuthors: Heiga Zen, Viet Dang, Rob Clark, Yu Zhang, Ron J. Weiss, Ye Jia, Zhifeng Chen, Yonghui Wu\nYear: 2019 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_dataset\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduced the LibriTTS corpus, a large 585-hour multi-speaker speech dataset specifically derived from LibriSpeech for text-to-speech applications.\n  2. Provided audio at a 24kHz sampling rate instead of 16kHz to support higher quality speech synthesis.\n  3. Segmented speech at sentence boundaries rather than silence intervals to preserve sentence-level prosody.\n  4. Included both original and normalized text to retain capitalization and punctuation useful for prosody modeling.\n  5. Added contextual information allowing access to neighboring sentences to facilitate inter-sentence prosody learning.\n  6. Filtered out utterances with significant background noise using estimated signal-to-noise ratios.\n  7. Demonstrated that neural TTS models trained on the LibriTTS corpus achieve high mean opinion scores in naturalness, outperforming models trained on the original LibriSpeech corpus.\n\nTags: TTS\n===\n2510.08392\n===\n2603.25727", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "514cf847-e2b2-4f3b-8d8a-d0cda909415c", "uuid": "1f0d117c-3ddd-45b3-b8bf-49ec4ce6aaab", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T07:43:32.878Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# LibriTTS: A Corpus Derived from LibriSpeech for Text-to-Speech\narXiv: 1904.02882\nAuthors: Heiga Zen, Viet Dang, Rob Clark, Yu Zhang, Ron J. Weiss, Ye Jia, Zhifeng Chen, Yonghui Wu\nYear: 2019 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_dataset\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduced the LibriTTS corpus, a large 585-hour multi-speaker speech dataset specifically derived from LibriSpeech for text-to-speech applications.\n  2. Provided audio at a 24kHz sampling rate instead of 16kHz to support higher quality speech synthesis.\n  3. Segmented speech at sentence boundaries rather than silence intervals to preserve sentence-level prosody.\n  4. Included both original and normalized text to retain capitalization and punctuation useful for prosody modeling.\n  5. Added contextual information allowing access to neighboring sentences to facilitate inter-sentence prosody learning.\n  6. Filtered out utterances with significant background noise using estimated signal-to-noise ratios.\n  7. Demonstrated that neural TTS models trained on the LibriTTS corpus achieve high mean opinion scores in naturalness, outperforming models trained on the original LibriSpeech corpus.\n\nTags: TTS\n===\n2510.08392\n===\n2603.25727", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
